{"candidates":[{"claim":"Resumption is permissible if and only if the event framing protocol deterministically isolates partial events as non-events, preserving physical append-only integrity while ignoring trailing bytes.","counterconditions":["If framing relies solely on length-prefixing without end-markers or checksums, partial events cannot be deterministically skipped, risking logical causal corruption.","If the resumption protocol actively mutates or deletes the partial trailing bytes to 'clean up' the log, physical append-only integrity is violated."],"mechanism":"The system relies on self-delimiting records or integrity checks to quarantine and skip partial trailing bytes after token-budget exhaustion. The append-only log is physically unchanged, satisfying the physical append-only integrity invariant.","optional_refs":["63a352ab84b75378e4d6e6005ac8dffa4783865efc9507c740960ff246f117ac","9caad82ebcbd83e7d9e12cd2f636bf3a83d405df118e5476e6a49a4668624635"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"claim":"Resumption is safe if and only if the exact causal fingerprint (program_hash, input_hash, model_checkpoint_hash) at resumption matches the run start, preventing logical divergence.","counterconditions":["If the system state changes between exhaustion and resumption (e.g., model updates, time-dependent inputs, or external state mutation), the fingerprint will mismatch and resumption is refused.","If the causal fingerprint cannot be computed because the system was interrupted mid-computation of the fingerprint itself."],"mechanism":"Resumption requires deterministic verification of the environment and inputs. If the causal fingerprint matches, the resumed stream matches the uninterrupted stream, satisfying logical causal continuity.","optional_refs":["74f020a3cdf000cdcc5ca1f6227de1705a0294feb60c2f0ae792ad351f96344b"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"claim":"If token-budget exhaustion is predictable, resumption is safe provided the system injects a synthetic barrier event before the forced crash.","counterconditions":["If exhaustion is abrupt and unpredictable (e.g., a hard kill or OOM), no synthetic barrier can be injected, and resumption must be refused.","If the synthetic barrier itself is corrupted because the warning was triggered too late, leaving the barrier event partial."],"mechanism":"A warning threshold (e.g., 90% budget) triggers a controlled suspension, emitting a synthetic barrier event that cleanly delimits the committed log up to that point.","optional_refs":["032d4125e6b7718672a0ccaeb0b97a669e9f311755b54dee0b235210851451d5"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"claim":"Resumption must always be refused after token-budget exhaustion; only a fresh re-execution from the last globally committed event is valid.","counterconditions":["If the system is strictly deterministic, stateless at the exhaustion point, and supports deterministic replay, resumption is logically indistinguishable from re-execution, though practically refused for safety.","If re-execution is impossible due to loss of original input state, this criterion implies the reasoning run is permanently lost."],"mechanism":"Any attempt to resume risks subtle state corruption that cannot be proven safe without a complete restart. The append-only log guarantees the history of the failed run, but a new run starts fresh from the last known good state.","optional_refs":["b0a3d571038c9314c15157a120d764f0c6da706958888a3f119a9a2d2f7c49df","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"claim":"Append-only identity is preserved by treating resumption as a new derived branch rather than a continuation, making resumption always permissible.","counterconditions":["If downstream consumers are not aware of the new branch identity and assume linear continuation, logical causal continuity is violated for those consumers.","If the system does not explicitly record the branch relationship, the causal history becomes fragmented and unintelligible."],"mechanism":"The original exhausted run remains untouched in the append-only log. The 'resumed' run is just a new run initialized with the state of the exhausted one, effectively creating a branch. No append-only violation occurs because the original stream is immutable.","optional_refs":["11082a195824e60914be82ed12caaad2160189e08cd5b0f62b7b6d7f3b44671e"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"claim":"Resumption is always safe at any point of exhaustion if the reasoning step is decomposed into idempotent, purely functional state transitions.","counterconditions":["If the reasoning step has side effects, external API calls, or non-deterministic randomness, idempotency fails and resumption risks branching outputs.","If the functional state transition itself relies on reading the partial event left by mid-stream exhaustion, which by definition lacks a complete state representation."],"mechanism":"Because each step is purely functional and idempotent, re-running or resuming from any point yields the exact same output. Append-only integrity is preserved because no prior event is mutated; resumption simply appends new identical events or continues deterministically.","optional_refs":["b7174837d23fe4c37e80cb3e27304ae050c0389249879df249e4658f3e79b6f3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"simulation_proposals":[]}